id: pm-ci-cancel-aggregate
title: Let superseded CI aggregators stop when their workflow is canceled
description: "CI run 40320157401 at 258ab2223 retained one queued Gates (coverage) job after all 24 other jobs completed and normal cancellation was accepted. The newer head 1a4b9a6f8 remained pending behind that concurrency slot. The aggregate used always(), which evaluates true even after workflow cancellation. Use the documented non-canceled status guard for the coverage and Windows aggregates, retaining failed/missing dependency admission and exact coverage requirements."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags: []
created_at: "2026-10-26T13:52:51.445Z"
updated_at: "2026-10-26T15:33:39.906Z"
closed_at: "2026-10-26T14:05:07.395Z"
completed_at: "2026-10-26T14:05:07.395Z"
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 30
acceptance_criteria: Both CI job aggregates stop on workflow cancellation; existing native shell admission controls still reject every non-success dependency outcome; full exact source coverage and all required contexts remain enforced.
parent: pm-u9d0
risk: low
confidence: high
resolution: "Use the documented !cancelled() job guard for the primary CI coverage and Windows aggregates. Retain existing dependency-result admission, exact global source coverage, permissions, runner budgets and every required context."
expected_result: Canceled obsolete workflows release their concurrency slot; active workflows still publish failing aggregate verdicts for every non-success dependency outcome.
actual_result: "Both unchanged always() guards fail the revised contract. All 24 workflow cases and native dependency outcome controls pass with the new guards; typecheck, build, ESLint and workflow permission checks pass. Hosted checks remain mandatory on the new head."
